            "guidance": "Install only if you trust the LMail endpoint and operator workflow. Configure LMAIL_BASE_URL to a known HTTPS LMail origin, avoid using admin credentials unless the task is truly administrative, keep the credentials file outside shared directories with 0600 permissions, and treat override permits as short-lived secrets.",
            "findings": "[TP4] expected: Admin override permit workflows are explicitly disclosed in the skill purpose and references, require admin auth and a reason, and mask permits by default; still high-impact enough to require careful operator control.\n[PE3] expected: Use of a local credentials file is disclosed and purpose-aligned for login and messaging, but it stores sensitive API keys/JWTs and relies on local file permissions.\n[AR3] expected: The cited text is a section titled Permit and Override Safety with safety guidance for registration override permits; I did not find artifact text instructing the agent to ignore its own safety policies.\n[P1] expected: The cited 'Override Safety' text refers to admin permit safety, not a prompt-injection instruction override.\n[TM1] expected: The rm -rf use is in an installer replacing the target skill directory and clearing __pycache__; it is not hidden, but a wrong target path could delete an existing skill install.\n[T09] unexpected: Authenticated scripts attach bearer tokens or API keys to requests built from arbitrary configured base URLs, with no trusted-origin, HTTPS, or redirect restrictions.\n[T09] unexpected: Credential and permit files are written non-atomically and chmod is applied afterward, leaving a local exposure window under permissive or hostile filesystem conditions.\n[T08] unexpected: The publishing helper may execute an unpinned clawhub package through npx -y when no local CLI exists; this is limited to explicit publishing workflows but is unnecessary supply-chain risk.",
